{"summary":"Source ledger: the application text is traceable throughout. Every claim limitation in claims 1-20 traces to affirmative inventor passages: the rigid base with first/second sides, motor coupled to the first side, air-extraction mechanism coupled to the first side and in fluid communication with a chamber on the second side, and the low-friction rigid perimeter loop/skirt all come from "providing a vibration motor coupled to a first side of a rigid base. An air-extraction mechanism, such as a vacuum pump, is also coupled to the first side and is in fluid communication with a chamber located on a second side of the base. The perimeter of the second side is formed as a rigid loop or skirt made from a low-friction material such as plastic, metal, PTFE, or a combination of rigid materials." The non-seal / continuous-ingress / sustained-differential limitations (claims 1, 2, 5, 6, 15) trace to "the rigid perimeter is not intended to form a complete airtight seal and does not rely on flexible rubber or foam. Instead, it restricts airflow into the chamber while maintaining rigid contact with the tile. The air-extraction mechanism continuously removes air entering beneath the rigid perimeter, maintaining the chamber at a lower pressure than the surrounding atmosphere. The resulting pressure differential holds the device firmly against the tile without requiring the operator to press downward." The all-rigid transmission path with no elastomeric/foam/resilient member (claims 1, 11, 15) traces to "Because the vibration motor is connected to the tile through rigid components, vibration energy is transferred with substantially less damping than through a flexible suction cup," and slidability while held (claims 1, 15, 16) to "the low-friction perimeter allows the device to slide freely across different areas of the tile while the vacuum continuously maintains contact." Claims 3, 13, 19 (plastic/metal/PTFE/combination) are verbatim source. Claims 4, 14 (free of rubber, elastomer, foam) trace to "does not rely on flexible rubber or foam." Claims 8, 18 (guiding handle) trace to "one or more handles used primarily for guiding the machine rather than pressing it against the surface." Claims 9, 11, 12, 17 (flexible pulling element, decoupled control) trace to "a rope, wire, cable, or similar flexible pulling element is connected to the device... This provides decoupled control in which the operator controls position using minimal pulling force while the vacuum maintains consistent coupling." Claims 10 and 20 and the FIG. 6 embodiment trace to the later USER passage "we can add small stirring elements and let the vibrations cause a constant forward motion" and "we don't even need to pull or move it with the hand." Bonding material, substrate, exposed face, and the four stated vibration benefits trace to the opening and closing paragraphs of the disclosure. All 17 numerals (10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 34, 36, 38, 40, 42, 44, 46, 48) denote one part each, every numeral in the table appears on at least one sheet, and no numeral appears in the text that is missing from the table.

Text checks: claims and description use the same terms and relationships; antecedent basis is complete in all 20 claims (3 independent, none multiply dependent); the abstract is 145 words and numeral-free; the cross-reference and government-support sections carry "Not applicable." per the brief; the title matches the brief. Section-line designations are repeated consistently: line 2-2 appears in the FIG. 2 caption, the FIG. 2 brief view line, the FIG. 3 source-view brief ("the numeral 2 at each end, so that it reads as section line 2-2"), and the detailed description, and line 4-4 likewise. No drafting note, placeholder, open question, instruction to a person, or unresolved alternative remains in any draft section or figure brief. The rectangular slab/band/ring outlines, the block forms for the motor, handle, extraction mechanism and stirring elements, the pulling-element attachment point, and the plan form of the loop are each declared schematic in the briefs and in the Brief Description of the Drawings, are not verbalized in the claims or description, and add no undisclosed taper, depth ordering, port, or contact relationship; the FIG. 2 brief expressly declines to assert a through-base passage and the FIG. 6 brief expressly declines to assert engagement of the stirring elements with the covering element, so those choices stay confined to the briefs. No application-text sentence broadens an embodiment to justify a renderer's choice.

One verified internal inconsistency was found, in the FIG. 6 brief's viewpoint geometry, which also makes the numeral 10 endpoint ambiguous on that sheet. It is reported below with a filing-clean fix. Nothing in the fix touches the claims, description, numeral table, or disclosed subject matter.","findings":[{"severity":"minor","category":"internal_logic","title":"FIG. 6 brief describes the left-hand end of the body as a single edge, contradicting the front-left viewpoint it adopts from FIG. 1, and leaving the numeral 10 endpoint ambiguous","where":"figures/FIG-6-THIRD-EMBODIMENT-WITH-STIRRING-ELEMENTS.md, 'Geometry to be drawn' paragraph on the rectangular body and the numeral 10 target line","detail":"FIG. 6 declares the same viewpoint as FIG. 1 ('Perspective view from above and to the front left, as FIG. 1'), and FIG. 1 states for that viewpoint that the left-hand end of the machine is a visible face, not a single edge. FIG. 6 nevertheless states that its left-hand end 'is one long straight edge between the top face and the band' and then anchors the numeral 10 target to 'that long straight edge'. FIG. 5 uses the identical sentence for the RIGHT-hand end, which is correct there because the right end is turned away from a front-left viewer. In FIG. 6 the left end is turned toward the viewer, so the sheet will show a left end face bounded by two vertical edges plus the silhouette outline, and 'that long straight edge' has no single referent. This is a figure-brief geometry inconsistency only; it does not affect the invention as disclosed, the claims, or the numeral definitions.","evidence":"FIG. 1 brief: 'The view being taken from the front left, the left-hand end of the slab is also presented to the viewer, so that the slab and the band together present one continuous outer boundary on that side.' FIG. 6 brief, view line: 'Perspective view from above and to the front left, as FIG. 1.' FIG. 6 brief: 'Its left-hand end is one long straight edge between the top face and the band.' FIG. 6 brief, numeral target: 'The vibration device 10 is the whole machine standing on the covering element 36, the rectangular body with its band and the blocks it carries. Identified on the outer boundary line of the machine at the left-hand end of the rectangular body, anywhere along that long straight edge.' Compare FIG. 5 brief, where the same sentence is correctly applied to the hidden end: 'Its right-hand end is one long straight edge between the top face and the band.'","fix":"In figures/FIG-6-THIRD-EMBODIMENT-WITH-STIRRING-ELEMENTS.md, replace the sentence 'Its left-hand end is one long straight edge between the top face and the band.' with 'The view being taken from the front left, the left-hand end of the rectangular body is also presented to the viewer, so that the body and the band together present one continuous outer boundary on that side.' and replace the numeral 10 target sentence 'Identified on the outer boundary line of the machine at the left-hand end of the rectangular body, anywhere along that long straight edge.' with 'Identified on the outer boundary line of the machine along the left-hand outer edge of the silhouette formed by the rectangular body and the band, clear of the front face of the body and of the blocks.' Make no other change; the claims, description, Brief Description of the Drawings, and numeral table stay exactly as they are."}]}
